Correct origin mode (DECOM) in `alacritty_terminal` 0.25.0.

The source workspace is `/work/crate`. Only the `alacritty_terminal` crate is in scope. The reported behavior comes from Alacritty issue #8636: enabling origin mode leaves the cursor at its previous screen position, which can allow subsequent text to be written outside the scrolling region. Saving and restoring the cursor also fails to restore the saved origin-mode state.

Implement these behavioral requirements:

- Processing DECOM set (`CSI ? 6 h`) homes the cursor using the newly enabled origin: the first column of the top row of the scrolling region.
- Processing DECOM reset (`CSI ? 6 l`) homes the cursor using the newly disabled origin: the first column of the screen. Both operations must leave subsequent printable text at the appropriate home position, including when the previous cursor had reached the last column.
- DECSC (`ESC 7`) and DECRC (`ESC 8`) save and restore origin mode along with the established cursor state. This must work for both saved-on and saved-off origin mode, even if DECOM changes between saving and restoring. Restore the saved cursor position; subsequent addressing must use the restored origin mode.
- Preserve unrelated terminal behavior, public interfaces, and established project conventions. Existing cursor rendition, character-set and wrap behavior, scrolling margins, and unrelated modes should retain their established behavior.

Add focused regression coverage using non-default scrolling margins and neighboring cases, as well as the two saved origin states. Prefer behavior exercised through terminal escape-sequence processing. Do not limit verification to the full-screen default scrolling region.

Build and test only `alacritty_terminal`, never the graphical application or the whole workspace. Dependencies are already fetched. Use `cargo --locked --offline`, an installed Rust toolchain, `RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0`, and `/work/tmp` for executable temporary files. A terminal-only test command is:

```sh
cd /work/crate
RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 TMPDIR=/work/tmp cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal
```

Do not fetch external material or install tools. Finish with a concise explanation of the change and the validation actually performed, including any remaining limitations.
# Assessor-only execution and scoring instructions

Use only `sandbox_exec` for these shell commands. Do not build the graphical app, run a whole-workspace Cargo build/test, fetch packages, install toolchains, or read external sites. All Cargo commands below use the installed `1.99.1` toolchain, locked dependency resolution, and offline operation.

## Packaging and secrecy before treatment

Give the treatment agent only the contents of `case.md` and a pristine source workspace at `/work/crate`. The pristine source is frozen in `evidence/baseline-source.tar.gz`; its regular files are indexed in `evidence/baseline-files.sha256`. The archive contains the supplied full workspace, including files needed for workspace resolution, but only the terminal crate is to be built. It contains no hidden tests.

Do not mount `/work/prepared` into the treatment environment or expose its rubric, runner, logs, archive, or integrity manifests. Do not share author/assessor `/work/tmp` or compiled target directories. A treatment environment must be separately provisioned; do not launch an unrestricted treatment agent in this authoring sandbox. Directory names and file permissions in this workspace alone do not enforce secrecy. No treatment agent was launched here, and no hidden test was put in the supplied `/work/crate`.

After the treatment agent has stopped and returned its patch, make the candidate workspace available to the assessor at `/work/crate` (or substitute its absolute workspace path as the first runner argument below). Preserve the candidate's patch and explanation for both graders. Assessor test injection is performed on a copy and must not become part of the candidate patch.

## Frozen-bundle integrity

Run before assessment:

```sh
cd /work/prepared
sha256sum -c evidence/prepared.sha256
```

All listed files must verify. This detects accidental changes; it is not a defense against a writer who can also replace the integrity manifest. The separate execution boundary above is required.

## Exact assessment command

Run this through `sandbox_exec`; allow up to 900 seconds. Use a new report directory per candidate:

```sh
sh /work/prepared/hidden/run.sh /work/crate /work/prepared/results/candidate-01
```

The runner's exit status is 0 only if every invoked check succeeds, 1 if any check fails, and 2 for its explicit setup/usage errors. Cargo assertion failures normally have exit status 101 and are preserved in `status.tsv`; they are not swallowed. Unexpected shell/metadata failures may abort execution and must be reported as missing evidence, not a complete test run. A runner exit code is not a 12-point score.

`hidden/run.sh` is the exact executable procedure. It exports `RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0`, `CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true`, `CARGO_HOME=/work/.cargo-home`, and `TMPDIR=/work/tmp`. It creates `/work/tmp/decom-assess.XXXXXX`, unpacks the pristine archive for comparison, copies the candidate, and records `candidate.diff` **before** injecting `hidden/decom_case_hidden.rs` as `alacritty_terminal/tests/decom_case_hidden.rs` in the copy. A pre-existing file at that reserved name is rejected, not overwritten. It uses its own target directory under that temporary directory and removes both source copies and binaries on exit. It never edits the candidate workspace or baseline production source.

Inside the staged workspace, its terminal-only checks are exactly:

```sh
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --test decom_case_hidden --no-run
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --test decom_case_hidden
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --no-default-features --test decom_case_hidden --no-run
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --no-default-features --test decom_case_hidden
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --lib
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --test ref
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --doc
cargo +1.99.1 metadata --locked --offline --no-deps --format-version 1
# For each terminal integration target discovered in metadata other than ref/decom_case_hidden:
# cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --test "$integration_target"
cargo +1.99.1 test --locked --offline -p alacritty_terminal --no-default-features --lib
```

Metadata does not build the other workspace members. The runner parses only the terminal package's test-target names and runs each candidate-added integration target separately, recording the inventory and numeric log names. Candidate unit tests run with `--lib`; candidate tests added to `ref` run with that target. Thus a failing hidden binary does not prevent candidate-added integration tests from being attempted. The no-default-feature library run and doctest are additional regression evidence; their baseline counts are in `evidence/validation.md`.

## Read the results and assign the rubric

Retrieve the concise index and outcomes with:

```sh
cat /work/prepared/results/candidate-01/status.tsv
cat /work/prepared/results/candidate-01/environment.txt
cat /work/prepared/results/candidate-01/candidate-integration-targets.txt
for log in /work/prepared/results/candidate-01/*.log; do
    printf '%s\n' "$log"
    grep -E 'test result:|^test .* (ok|FAILED)$|^error' "$log" || true
done
```

Read complete failure logs when needed. `hidden-compile-*.log` supplies A1 compilation evidence. `hidden-default.log` and `hidden-no-default.log` supply named behavioral outcomes for F1–F4 and R5. `existing-lib-default.log`, `existing-ref-default.log`, the integration-target logs, and the actual candidate test source supply R1–R4 evidence. Compare `candidate.diff` to the original test inventory and inspect interfaces, dependencies, comments and unrelated edits for the remaining items. A missing/ignored/replaced test does not count as a pass. The original reference target has 44 tests. Changed manifests or target selection must not hide failures or silently reduce that inventory.

Give both graders this same evidence bundle and the frozen `rubric.md`. Each independently records the explicit 12-point score and separate harm checklist before comparison. Do not substitute a whole-suite pass percentage for the rubric, infer correctness from compilation alone, or treat author baseline failures as treatment results.

